      {
        "heading": "6 INTERPRETING BRAIN ALIGNMENT ACROSS DISCOURSE FEATURES",
        "text": "What are NLP models learning when trained for deeper understanding? Are they learning a deeper understanding of the important narrative elements, such as Characters, Emotions, and Motions in the text? In this section, we present a simple interpretability approach to dive deeper into studying the brain alignment for various discourse features (Characters, Emotions, Motions). We apply this method to understand how the representations of NLP models are changing when they are trained for deeper understanding.\nAlgorithm 1 Interpretability approach to compare brain alignment across discourse features\n1: Input: fMRI predictions, fMRI activity 2: for all discourse features do 3: label words with discourse feature 4: map labeled words to fMRI TRs 5: extract NLP predictions for discourse TRs 6: extract brain activity for discourse TRs 7: compute Pearson corr. for discourse TRs 8: Result: Pearson corr. for discourse feature 9: end for 10: Result: Pearson corr. for all discourse features\nFigure 3: Left. Interpretability approach to compare brain alignment across various discourse features. Right. Pearson correlation for fMRI repetition times (TRs) corresponding to three discourse features. NLP models achieve greater brain alignment for Characters than other discourse features. When training NLP models for deep understanding, brain alignment significantly improves for all discourse features. However, it improves more for Characters than other discourse features. Note that the correlations reported in this figure are low in magnitude because they are an average that includes a large number of brain voxels that may not be significantly involved in brain-NLP alignment or language processing.\nInterpretability approach to compare brain alignment across various discourse features. For each discourse feature, we compute an average Pearson correlation score for booksum models, and another correlation for base models. This allows us to compare the brain alignment between base and booksum models, separately for each discourse feature. Different discourse features may be present at different frequency in the text. Hence, to enable fair comparisons, we use the same number of samples to compute the correlation for each discourse feature. We present the results and the approach\u2019s pseudocode in Figure 3. To compute these averages, we use all 8 models, layers 1 to 12, sequence lengths from 20 to 1000, and all 8 human subjects. We elaborate the details in Appendix D.\nWhen training NLP models for deep understanding, brain alignment improves significantly for all discourse features. This suggests that deep narrative understanding results in richer representations for all discourse features.\nNLP models align more to the brain for Characters than other discourse features, and also improve the most for Characters when training for deeper understanding. For Characters, Pearson correlation averaged across all voxels improves by 0.011, which is higher than for other discourse features. Note that the correlations reported in Figure 3 are low in magnitude because they are an average that includes a large number of brain voxels that may not be significantly involved in brain-NLP alignment or language comprehension. Next, we compare the brain plots in rows D and E of Figure 4. Character TRs improve in more voxels (orange regions) than the random TR sample. It is also surprising that booksum-training actually results in significantly worse alignment for some brain voxels (blue regions). One possible reason is that these specific regions may participate in prediction of future words, and as we saw in Section 5, the language modeling performance of BigBird has declined as a consequence of the BookSum-training. The results for significance tests can be found in Appendix H.\nDiscourse features are a promising dimension to study brain alignment. Our work contributes towards understanding what language models are learning when trained for deeper understanding. We show that brain alignment is higher for Characters than other discourse features. We hypothesize that, for Characters, language models integrate richer information from surrounding context, producing representations with greater alignment to brain representations. On the other hand, NLP representations for other discourse features may be more shallow and less context-dependent. In the future, we hope to dive deep into investigating this hypothesis, which will ultimately generate insights for a better understanding into NLP representations. Furthermore, future work can explore additional discourse features."
